## Step 1: Understand the problem
We are tasked with finding the matrix that corresponds to projecting onto the $y$-axis. This means we want to find the matrix that, when multiplied by any vector, will give us the orthogonal projection of that vector onto the $y$-axis.

## Step 2: Recall the general form of the projection matrix
The projection matrix onto a vector (or in this case, the $y$-axis) has a specific form, given by a matrix that projects any vector onto a fixed vector by "swapping" the vector with its component in the direction of the fixed vector, and scaling it appropriately. For the $y$-axis, the projection matrix onto this axis can be represented in 2D by a matrix form that applies this transformation.

## Step 3: Determine the fixed vector for the projection
For projecting onto the $y$-axis, the fixed vector is $(0, 1)$, since it aligns parallel to the $y$-axis.

## Step 4: Construct the projection matrix
The projection matrix $P$ onto the $y$-axis, given the fixed vector $(0, 1)$, can be constructed by realizing that any vector projected into this line will lose its x-component. A projection matrix in 2D is given as:
\[ P = \frac{1}{1^2} \left(
\begin{array}{cc}
\mathbf{0} & \mathbf{0} \\
\mathbf{0} & 1
\end{array}
\right)
\]
which simplifies to:
\[ P = \left(
\begin{array}{cc}
0 & 0 \\
0 & 1
\end{array}
\right)
\]

## Step 5: Solve for the matrix
The matrix $P$ is identified as the projection matrix that corresponds to projecting a vector onto the $y$-axis. No multiplication or transformation is necessary; the scalar value was not applied as the problem statement refers to a transformation that happens in a general form, so we simply accept the constructed matrix as it applies.

