Solve the system of equations. The prompt text says to distribute, aggregate, or substitute to find the solution.

$ \begin{cases} x +2y + 3z = -2 \\ x - 2y + 7z = 11 \end{cases}$

The first equation is multiplied by $-1$. This gives the following system:

$ \begin{cases} -x - 2y - 3z = 2 \\ x -2y + 7z = 11 \end{cases}$

If the equations are added together, the sum of two of the terms $x, 2y ,$ ~and $3z$ will cancel. This gives
$$\begin{array}{  c c c c c }
-x-x~~~~\ & &-2y-2y~~~~\ & & 7z+(-3z) \\

\hline
-2x &  & -4y & &  4z \\
\end{array}$$so that the combined equation is $-2x-4y+4z=13$. In order to isolate the variable we begin asking if we can combine this with any of the original equations. We can  combine the equation that we have just obtained with the first equation by adding that equation to it, when we perform the addition, the term containing the variable $3z$ is cancelled out again as is one term containing variable $x$. Therefore the combined equation is $-4y+4z=15$, and dividing by common factor of $4$ yields $-y+z=\frac{15}{4}.$ \\

The prompt text asked to use a method of distribute, aggregate, or substitute. Since we used add to form the equation $-2x-4y+4z=13, $  this is  a kind of aggregate.\\
 We can combine this new equation with one of the original equations by adding to write  the new equation

$ \begin{cases} 
-2x-4y+4z= 13 \\
 x+2y+3z= -2 \\
 \end{cases}   $    ~---
Then we have 
 $\begin{array}{ c |c |c |c |c |}
  & -2x &  -4y &  +4z & =13 \\
  + &  x  &  +2y &  +3z & = -2 \\
\hline 
 & -x &  -2y &  +7z & = 11 \\
  \end{array}$   When we add these two equations together, we cancel out the $y$'s and $4z$'s and find that the combined equation is  $-x+7z=11$. In order to isolate the variable we begin asking if we can combine this with any of the original equations. We can combine the equation that we have just obtained with the first equation by adding that equation to it, when we perform the addition, the term containing the variable $2y$ is cancelled out again as is one term containing variable $x$. Therefore the combined equation is $-15+7z=24$, and by adding 15$ to both sides of the equation we get that $7z=39$, from this the unknown $z$ can be deduced to be $\frac{39}{7}$.\\

We can substitute this into either of the equations above to obtain a solution.
 If we choose the first equation above we find

\begin{align*}
 x &  +2y + 3z= -2 \\
 \Rightarrow\qquad x & + 2y  + \frac{3\cdot39}{7} =-2\\
 \Rightarrow\qquad x &+\frac{78}{7} + 2y =\frac{-14}{7}\\
 \end{align*} 
that can be simplified by dividing every term by 7$.

\begin{align*}
 x &+ 12 + 2y =-2\\ 
  \Rightarrow\qquad x + 2y &=-2-12 \\
 \Rightarrow\qquad x + 2y &=-14 \\
\end{align*}

At this point we can see the end result of our ability to choose to either isolate  a  variable in an equation, or eliminate all the variables from the equations , which we refer to as *a complete solution.*  Our ability of eliminating the variable $\boxed{x}$ has allowed for a complete solution of the equation, and our ability to properly substitute in the expression for variable $z$ has allowed us to eliminate $z$ as well.\\

